In the world of security auditing and bug bounty hunting, when we talk about "bypassing a status code," we mean tricking the web application or intermediate proxy layers (like a reverse proxy, WAF, or CDN) into turning a restriction status code into a successful access state (200 OK). [1] 
The primary status codes targeted for bypasses are authentication and authorization flags: 403 Forbidden, 401 Unauthorized, and 404 Not Found (when used to hide administrative portals). [2, 3, 4] 
Below is the complete, advanced blueprint of how these status codes are bypassed using specific HTTP input manipulations.
------------------------------
## 1. Bypassing 403 Forbidden & 401 Unauthorized
These codes mean the server or proxy is explicitly blocking you from accessing a path (like /admin). Attackers target the communication gap between the proxy (which handles routing) and the backend server (which processes the code). [5] 
## Technique A: HTTP Header Override (Tricking the Proxy) [6] 
Reverse proxies often check the URL path to enforce blocks, but they allow custom headers to override the destination. If the proxy only inspects the main URL block but passes custom headers straight through, the backend will route you to the restricted page. [7, 8, 9] 

* The Target Link: https://example.local (Returns 200)
* The Injection Headers: Add headers specifying the administrative directory location:

X-Original-URL: /admin
X-Override-URL: /admin
X-Rewrite-URL: /admin

* The Result: The proxy allows the request because it thinks you are visiting the root path /, but the backend processes the header and serves the /admin console. [10, 11, 12, 13] 

## Technique B: IP Whitelist Spoofing (Tricking Location Controls)
Some administrative folders throw a 403 Forbidden unless the request comes from an internal company IP address (like 127.0.0.1 or a private local subnet). You can spoof your origin address by adding routing metadata headers. [14, 15, 16, 17] 

* The Headers to Inject:

X-Forwarded-For: 127.0.0.1
X-Originating-IP: 127.0.0.1
X-Remote-IP: 127.0.0.1
Client-IP: 127.0.0.1
True-Client-IP: 127.0.0.1

[18, 19, 20, 21] 

## Technique C: Path Traversal and Obfuscation
If a firewall blocks access to /admin, you can alter the path syntax using special characters. If the proxy evaluates the string literally but the backend server normalizes the directory dots, you bypass the block. [22] 

* URL Variations to Test:
* https://example.local (Semicolon trick common in Java/Tomcat apps)
   * https://example.local
   * https://example.local
   * https://example.local.
   * https://example.local (URL-encoding the dot character)

## Technique D: HTTP Method Toggling (Verb Tampering) [23] 
A application might protect /admin by strictly blocking GET requests, but forget to block alternative request methods. [24] 

* The Fix: Swap the HTTP verb in your terminal request tool or Burp Suite to options like POST, PUT, PATCH, or HEAD while keeping the path identical. [25, 26, 27, 28] 

